u have those choices it's great  that we have places like food cycle and  social bites for people to come to and  we're absolutely thrilled to be  supported as well by  um the just eat winter meal appeal where  you know people  um have been incredibly generous  actually  um they have donated the equivalent of  385  000 meals to this campaign which uh Josh  and I are absolutely thrilled with  that's fantastic absolutely fantastic  but I was just about to ask a question  on that I mean Josh look I I don't care  who you are I mean unless you're a  multi-millionaire you know all of us  have probably made changes to our life  you know as we run into Christmas  because of the cost of living crisis  everything's so much more expensive you  know my energy bills are going through  the roof does that mean even at a time  like this that you start to see perhaps  the the level of donation the level of  support that you get from the public  tapering away what going by what Mary  was saying not actually  well I mean you know I've been  um running social baiting over the last  10 years and it's always amazes me  um at times of Crisis and Society at  times of economic hardship you would  absolutely expect donations to taper in  but it's always in my experience so  inspiring to see that it's actually the  reverse and people sometimes without  very much and get you know give to those  that are even more in need and I think  they just eat was to Winter meal appeal  really demonstrates that I think I  understand they've had three times as  many people donating and the way it  works is when you go to the checkout and  you're ordering a takeaway and just eat  you can donate either a pound and five  pounds or ten pounds and this year  compared to last year they've had over  three times as many people than 18 and  Mary said that's helped to and fund to  that to this point  um over 385 000 hot meals which is going  to be delivered over Christmas and right  through winter and we're hoping that  those Nations keep going let them up to  the end of the year and hopefully we can  hit the half a million meal Target  do you know what it is the time of year  for giving thanks isn't it so you can  both have mine for all the work that you  do uh Mary McGrath see your food cycle  uh Josh Littlejohn founder of social  bike keep up the good work and have a  lovely Christmas when it comes you too  Niall thank you thank you thanks so much  Christmas songs well it can certainly  lift your spirits but for people living  with Parkinson's stroke or brain injury  Christmas music can do much more than  that helping them with communication  projection and even motor function  that's why songwriter Madeleine castry  has written an original Christmas song  sung entirely by those living and  affected by neurological disabilities  let's have a list  Christmas songs  [Music]  the songs writer and the founder of the  charity resonate Madeleine Cass Street  Madeleine great to see you this morning  I do hope you're well um just just tell  me a little bit about how this this song  came about obviously you have you have  written it but performance duties have  been handed over  well this project was fundamentally born  out of my belief that music really can  change lives and over after overcoming  illness myself as a teenager I began to  fundraise and I was using my musical  background to Stage little local  concerts and that all began over 10  years ago and this then coincided with  me meeting a wonderful lady living with  Parkinson's and she was a huge huge  lover of singing so I began to think  about how the classical singing  technique I was trained with could be  made a little bit more accessible to  help her live with her condition and  this snowballed into working with  broader Parkinson's support groups and  now some wonderful groups made up of  those living with the effects of stroke  and brain injury and this song is the  result it really is a shared effort to  show that music really really can change  lives I mean what is fascinating to me  and I've done I know I think or two  about Parkinson's what fascinates me is  of course getting together and singing  everyone understands the benefits of  something that that raises the spirit  you make friends you have a laugh but  the idea that singing like this can  bring people together and actually have  you know an effect on on motor skills of  course one of the one of the problems  with Parkinson's is that degrading kind  of motor skill that you have that is  that is fascinating to me tell me a  little bit more  well whenever I approached a project  like this I'm always mindful of the  different symptoms that can present  themselves so for instance Parkinson's  has over 40 different symptoms and a lot  of people just think of Parkinson's as  the shake but actually it isn't there's  so many many different symptoms and of  course stroke is different for every  person but one thing that you might not  consider is how this can affect the  voice so breathing perception of volume  Clarity of words these are all impacted  by neurological disease and the voices  obviously our main method of  communication so it must be so isolating  when that is compromised by a  neurological disability so the songs  that I write are made up of motifs that  assist in phonation I've considered  things like breath support can you can  you just explain that sorry for it's  very early on a Monday morning you'll  have to explain the terminology  Phoenician what do you mean there the  nation so it's basically how you form  your words and obviously when you lose  the ability to access certain muscles as  easily particularly in the cases of  stroke it's very important that we  engage to what we call the articulators  so things that move in the mouth and  these can be starting with very small  exercises like imagining you're chewing  a toffee and it's getting bigger in your  mouth and but then obviously you put  this into phrase and I think that  sometimes one of the real big stumbling  blocks with neurological conditions is  that spontaneity that we have in speech  but we don't necessarily get that with  music because we have the beats and the  Rhythm to help us so what I've tried to  do with these songs is create little  phrases and they're not just uplifting  messages of Hope which I hope that  everybody can resonate with but they're  also little pocket motifs that people  can use when maybe they freeze or they  stumble over their words to get their  articulators to get their face moving  and really really help them improve  their quality of life and of course it  strikes me you know you're classically  trained yourselves I mean the one thing  the classically trained singers never  have to worry about is their posture  posture of course and another issue for  a number of those kind of neurological  conditions just wondering though of the  people that you have been interacting  with and in producing this single what  have they been saying to you I mean I  was looking at the video there and  everyone seems to be having a ball  well I don't necessarily think that they  realize the effect that music can have  on them so it really really is a case  where I've been working with people and  their partners and spouses have come up  to me and said this is the first time  they've spoken coherently in years  because all of a sudden they've got  access to this breath support that they  weren't necessarily using before and  also sometimes something so you know we  wouldn't necessarily think it has such  power but speaking to a beat using a  familiar Soul or even a little made-up  Rhythm to articulate that speech and to  give you that kind of signposting  through a phrase it just gives you that  new sense of coherency and I think above  all else there's nothing more powerful  than giving the gift of voice and  communication particularly at Christmas  and these wonderful courageous people  have been you know they've been  effectively silenced by these  neurological conditions and such cruel  diseases and to be able to get them to  communicate again through music well  that's totally Priceless Madeline we are  almost out of time in the program but so  tell us where can we get hold of the  single uh well the single is available  on all streaming platforms and if you  want to have a look at the charity  website which is  resonatemusic.org.uk there's lots of  support materials on there to get  everybody involved in using music and  singing for health and wellness and  those living with disability and those  who just want to use music to spread all  on their website  um Madeline it's been an absolute  pleasure having you on the program wish  you all the very best with the song and  look have a lovely Christmas when it  comes happy Christmas
 

